          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>One dot per industry, plotted twice: once for its share of all factory jobs, once for its share of all factory value added. If jobs and value tracked each other, every dot would sit on the diagonal. They do not. The dots below it carry more workers than their output warrants: the labour-intensive backbone. The dots above carry more output than their headcount: the capital-intensive core.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>The most labour-intensive industry is <b id="ex-jv-lab">–</b>; the most capital-intensive <b id="ex-jv-cap">–</b>. A factory job in the one is not the same as a factory job in the other, and the aggregate productivity number hides the gap between them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-lab" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Where the factory jobs are</h3>
