          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-wage"></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, ASI (NIC 2008): total emoluments (wages, salaries, bonus and the employer's contributions) divided by total persons engaged, computed by India Atlas from the two published series. Persons engaged include managers, supervisors and unpaid working owners, so this is an average over everyone on the rolls, not a worker's wage</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Divide the year's wage bill by the number of people on the rolls and you get what an average job in that industry is worth. Divide by twelve for a month. The low end is the work India does by hand: rolling bidis, cutting and stitching cloth, tanning leather, sawing wood. The high end is capital-heavy plants where a few people run expensive machines, and where a large share of the bill goes to engineers and managers. The same factory floor can hold both: these are averages over everyone, from the helper to the plant head.</p>
