            <div id="plot-sec-wrk"></div>
            <div id="plot-sec-gpw" style="margin-top:26px"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, ASUSE, broad activity groups (Manufacturing, Trading, Other Services) by rural/urban sector, published aggregates, not computed, GVA per worker refers to market establishments, nominal</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each row is one activity; the line connects the rural value (grey dot) to the urban value (blue dot). The top panel counts workers; the bottom panel values them. Read the two panels together: where the workers sit and where the value sits are different answers.</p>
            <p>Manufacturing workers skew rural: the village workshop, the handloom. Trade and services skew urban. But GVA per worker skews urban in every activity: the city establishment adds more per head than the rural one, whether it makes, trades or serves. The gap is widest in manufacturing and narrowest in services.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>Rural India holds <b id="ex-sec-estr">–</b> of informal establishments and <b id="ex-sec-wrkr">–</b> of informal workers; urban India holds <b id="ex-sec-estu">–</b> of establishments and <b id="ex-sec-wrku">–</b> of workers. Yet urban GVA per worker is <b id="ex-sec-gap">–</b> the rural level in manufacturing, narrowing to <b id="ex-sec-gaps">–</b> in services.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

