            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The factory GVA strip earlier in this guide concentrates in three western states. This mirror, informal workers, not factory value, spreads wider. Uttar Pradesh, West Bengal and Maharashtra hold the largest shares, but the tail is long: states that barely register in factory output carry millions of informal workers each.</p>
            <p>The comparison is the point. A state's rank here against its rank on the factory strip measures how much of its economy the ASI misses: and for most states, that is most of the economy.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>The largest is <b id="ex-ust1">–</b>. The top three states together hold <b id="ex-ust3">–</b> of informal workers; the top five, <b id="ex-ust5">–</b>. Against the factory GVA top three's share, concentration here is markedly lower.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <script type="application/json" id="u25-data" set:html={JSON.stringify({ all: u25All, f: u25F, trades })}></script>
