            <div class="sub">Workers in unincorporated non-farm enterprises by detailed activity, calendar year 2025 (the new ASUSE round), all {trades.length} activities the survey publishes, sorted</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-trades">–</div><div class="c">informal workers, 2025, crore</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-trades"></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO, ASUSE 2025 (calendar year), table 26, estimated workers by activity, all genders, full and part time. The 2025 round covers January to December, a different reference period from the financial-year rounds of 2021-22 to 2023-24, so its levels are shown on their own</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>These are the shops, stalls, workshops and service counters that are not companies: the kirana store, the tailor, the auto-rickshaw owner, the tuition centre, the roadside dhaba. Each bar counts the people who work in them, owners and helpers included. Retail is the giant because a shop is the easiest business in India to open: little capital, often no registration at all, frequently run from the front room of a house.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2025</span>
            <p>The 2025 round counts {lakhN(u25All)} workers in all. The next three trades after {lc(trades[0].a)}, {lc(trades[1].a)}, {lc(trades[2].a)} and {lc(trades[3].a)}, hold {lakhN(nextThree)} together. The last financial-year round, 2023-24, counted {lakhN(fyWorkers)}; the two rounds cover different twelve months and are not one series, so the gap between them is not a measure of growth.</p>
