  <!-- § 09 the informal firm, described -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>The informal firm, described</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">ASUSE, per 1,000 establishments, 2023-24</span></div>
    <p>Counting the informal economy was the first act; describing it is the second. ASUSE also asks each establishment who owns it, where it stands, how long it runs and what paperwork it holds: answers published as rates per 1,000 establishments. They describe a firm that is one person, male-owned, run from home or a permanent stall, open all year: and mostly outside every register the state keeps. Of every 1,000 in 2023-24, <b id="if-reg">–</b> were registered under no act at all, and <b id="if-comp">–</b> used a computer.</p>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:44px">
      <figure id="cb-owner" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Who owns the shop</h3>
            <div class="sub">Proprietary &amp; partnership establishments, per 1,000, by owner's social group &amp; by sex of owner, 2023-24, All India</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-owner">–</div><div class="c">owned by OBC, SC or ST proprietors, per 1,000</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div class="basket" style="padding:0 0 8px">
              <div class="bar" id="bar-social"></div>
              <div class="labels" id="lab-social"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="basket" style="padding:28px 0 8px">
              <div class="bar" id="bar-sex"></div>
              <div class="labels" id="lab-sex"></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, ASUSE indicators 5, 6 &amp; 3, per-1,000 distributions as published, combined sector, proprietary &amp; partnership establishments only, a company has no single owner to classify</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The informal economy is where India's less-favoured groups own things. The owner distribution leans further toward OBC and SC proprietors than the ownership of registered companies ever has, and that is the quiet function of this sector: it is the open door. The bar below it shows the other gate: sex. Most establishments are one proprietor, and most proprietors are men; female-owned establishments cluster in manufacturing, largely home-based work.</p>
            <p>Education completes the portrait: the modal owner has schooling between primary and secondary. Graduates are a minority of owners here: the graduate premium in India is a wage premium, collected in jobs, not shops.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>Per 1,000 proprietary and partnership establishments: <b id="ex-o-obc">–</b> OBC-owned, <b id="ex-o-sc">–</b> SC-owned, <b id="ex-o-st">–</b> ST-owned. Female proprietors run <b id="ex-o-fem">–</b> of every 1,000 proprietary establishments. Owners educated below secondary school: <b id="ex-o-edu">–</b> per 1,000.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-modern" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The paperwork and the wires</h3>
            <div class="sub">Establishments per 1,000 with a bank account, a computer, an internet connection, a registration, 2023-24, All India, combined</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-modern">–</div><div class="c">per 1,000 using the internet, all establishments</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-modern"></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, ASUSE indicators 9, 20 &amp; 22, rates per 1,000 establishments as published, registration rates are not exclusive, one establishment can hold several, GST = CGST Act registration</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Two Indias share this strip, and one surprise. Bank accounts are the reached frontier: three establishments in four keep one, a decade of financial-inclusion policy visible in a single dot. The computer never arrived: a few dozen per thousand, flat across all three rounds. The surprise is the internet dot between them, which nearly doubled in two years: the smartphone became a business tool without a computer ever entering the shop.</p>
            <p>The registration dots are the formality frontier. An establishment registered under the Shops Act or GST is still unincorporated: registration is a spectrum, not a switch, and most of this economy sits at the unregistered end of it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>Per 1,000 establishments: <b id="ex-m-bank">–</b> maintain a bank or post-office account, <b id="ex-m-net">–</b> use the internet, <b id="ex-m-comp">–</b> a computer. <b id="ex-m-gst">–</b> hold GST registration, <b id="ex-m-shops">–</b> a Shops &amp; Establishments registration: and <b id="ex-m-none">–</b> are registered under nothing. Hiring changes the picture: internet use is <b id="ex-m-hwe">–</b> per 1,000 among hired-worker establishments against <b id="ex-m-oae">–</b> among own-account ones.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-rhythm" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Where it stands, when it works</h3>
            <div class="sub">Location of the establishment, months operated in the year, hours in the day, per 1,000, 2023-24</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-rhythm">–</div><div class="c">per 1,000 operating more than 9 months a year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div class="basket" style="padding:0 0 8px">
              <div class="bar" id="bar-loc"></div>
              <div class="labels" id="lab-loc"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="basket" style="padding:28px 0 8px">
              <div class="bar" id="bar-months"></div>
              <div class="labels" id="lab-months"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="basket" style="padding:28px 0 8px">
              <div class="bar" id="bar-hours"></div>
              <div class="labels" id="lab-hours"></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, ASUSE indicators 10, 14 &amp; 16, per-1,000 distributions as published, combined sector, all establishment types, street vendors are establishments without fixed premises</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The picture of precarity that "informal" suggests, a street vendor, seasonal, a few hours a day, is the minority case. Most establishments have a roof and an address: household premises or a permanent structure outside one. Most run more than nine months a year, and the modal working day is a full eight-to-eleven hours. Informal describes the paperwork, not the work.</p>
            <p>The margins are still where the hardship lives. The mobile and street-vending sliver at the end of the top bar has no premises to return to, and the short-months establishments at the left of the middle bar are the seasonal trades: brick work, sugarcane crushing, festival goods.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>Per 1,000 establishments: <b id="ex-r-house">–</b> operate from within the household and <b id="ex-r-perm">–</b> from a permanent structure outside it; street vending and mobile trade account for <b id="ex-r-street">–</b>. <b id="ex-r-m9">–</b> operate more than nine months a year; <b id="ex-r-h8">–</b> work an eight-to-eleven-hour day. The average establishment operates <b id="ex-r-avg">–</b> months in the year.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
